# JavaScript code to remove "(age X)" pattern

Here are several solutions to remove the "(age X)" pattern from a string:

## Solution 1: Using Regular Expression (Recommended)

```javascript
function removeAge(text) {
  return text.replace(/\s*\(age\s+\d+\)\s*/gi, ' ').trim();
}

// Test
const input = "july 7 1994 (age 7) London, UK (1994-07-07)";
const result = removeAge(input);
console.log(result);
// Output: "july 7 1994 London, UK (1994-07-07)"
```

## Solution 2: More Robust Pattern (handles variations)

```javascript
function removeAge(text) {
  // Removes (age X) where X can be any number (including multi-digit)
  return text.replace(/\s*\(age\s+\d+\)\s*/gi, ' ').replace(/\s+/g, ' ').trim();
}

// Test cases
console.log(removeAge("july 7 1994 (age 7) London, UK"));
// Output: "july 7 1994 London, UK"

console.log(removeAge("january 1 1950 (age 73) New York"));
// Output: "january 1 1950 New York"

console.log(removeAge("march 15 2000 (AGE 23) Paris"));
// Output: "march 15 2000 Paris"
```

## Solution 3: One-liner Arrow Function

```javascript
const removeAge = (text) => text.replace(/\s*\(age\s+\d+\)\s*/gi, ' ').trim();

console.log(removeAge("july 7 1994 (age 7) London, UK (1994-07-07)"));
// Output: "july 7 1994 London, UK (1994-07-07)"
```

## Explanation of the Regex Pattern:

- `\s*` - Matches zero or more whitespace characters before the parenthesis
- `\(` - Matches the opening parenthesis (escaped)
- `age` - Matches the literal text "age"
- `\s+` - Matches one or more whitespace characters
- `\d+` - Matches one or more digits (handles any age number)
- `\)` - Matches the closing parenthesis (escaped)
- `\s*` - Matches zero or more whitespace characters after the parenthesis
- `g` - Global flag (replaces all occurrences)
- `i` - Case-insensitive flag (matches "age", "Age", "AGE", etc.)

